DOLLY 7
PACKAGE rust
REQUIRES HOST runtime@0
REQUIRES HOST dso@0
REQUIRES HOST threads@0

# The Rust SDK as rust-build built it. Its rustc finds its sysroot beside
# itself, so /usr/bin/rustc starts it by its own path.
COPY /v0.1.4/demos/rust/Dollyfile-rust-build 38de43ed67917ffb9572bef9083f4a40347a9dcd3ec1bb91187e044c82d9e366 /opt/rust-sdk /opt/rust-sdk
COPY /v0.1.4/demos/rust/Dollyfile-rust-build 38de43ed67917ffb9572bef9083f4a40347a9dcd3ec1bb91187e044c82d9e366 /usr/share/licenses/rust /usr/share/licenses/rust
# rustc links what it compiles with the C toolchain.
INSTALL /v0.1.4/Dollyfile-cc 1e00616eeda62fce8d1e5198534152a32cc50e6452f8004a0f4f9ce4c190efd3
FILE /usr/bin/rustc
    #!/bin/slop
    /opt/rust-sdk/bin/rustc "$@"
EXPORTS TOOL rustc
EXPORTS FOLDER rust-sdk /opt/rust-sdk
EXPORTS FOLDER rust-licenses /usr/share/licenses/rust
FILE /usr/share/man/cat1/rustc.1
    rustc: the Rust compiler and standard library, built inside Dolly from their
    pinned sources (Rust 1.98.1, its LLVM 22.1.8).
    
      rustc main.rs -o main     compiles a program; cc links it
      cargo build               builds a crate (the cargo package)
    
    Programs are compiled with panics that abort, and there are no dynamic Rust
    libraries. Executables link threaded and need threads@0 in the image that
    runs them. The compiler loads procedural macros, so an image that keeps
    rustc declares dso@0. Upstream's documentation: https://doc.rust-lang.org/
